# Files owned by the user — fern generate will not overwrite these.
cli/hedra/custom.rs
README.md

# NOTE: src/pager.rs is deliberately NOT listed (ENG-10250). It held a hand-carried
# `#[serial]` fix for the flaky PAGER env-var tests; at the pinned generator (0.30.2)
# the emitted file is byte-identical, so the entry only risked silently reverting the
# next upstream fix. Safe to un-ignore because the generator DOES emit it — unlike
# .gitattributes below. Re-measure before re-adding.

# Cargo.toml stays generator-owned (#25) — fern-config sets `packageIdentity`, so
# regeneration writes hedra-cli's crate identity. Cargo.lock does NOT: the
# generator emits one, but it is resolved from a stale snapshot rather than from
# the manifests it ships in the same run. #29 emitted a lockfile missing `chrono`
# — a direct dependency of an unmodified hedra-sdk/Cargo.toml — along with the
# whole gzip transitive set, so `cargo check --locked` refused it. Pinning the
# lockfile here keeps the good resolution instead of letting each regeneration
# clobber it. (ENG-10048 tried the opposite in #27; this is that experiment's
# result, not a return to an untested assumption.)
#
# Known edge, ENG-10130: the generator stamps the release version into both
# Cargo.toml and Cargo.lock, so a version bump moves the un-ignored manifest and
# not this pinned lockfile — refresh with `cargo fetch` when that happens.
Cargo.lock

# Release pipeline (cargo-dist) + CI + Dependabot are hand-maintained.
dist-workspace.toml
.github/

# The generator never emits .gitattributes — which is exactly why this entry has
# to stay. For a file that exists in the repo but is not emitted, .fernignore is
# the only thing stopping regeneration from DELETING it: PR #29 removed the file
# outright once the entry was dropped. "Never emitted" makes an entry
# load-bearing, not redundant; only a file that is both unemitted and absent is
# safe to un-ignore.
.gitattributes
.gitignore

# The wire-contract harness. The generator never emits `tests/`, which is
# exactly why this entry is load-bearing: for a file that exists but is not
# emitted, .fernignore is the only thing stopping regeneration from DELETING
# it (see the .gitattributes entry above and PR #29).
tests/

# Hand-owned Replay config (ENG-10241) — the .gitattributes shape again, and
# the worst case of it: hand-written, never emitted, and — unlike
# .fern/replay.lock — never rewritten by Replay either, so nothing would put it
# back. Fern's own CLI agrees the entry belongs here: REPLAY_FERNIGNORE_ENTRIES
# hard-codes [.fern/replay.lock, .fern/replay.yml, .gitattributes], and Replay's
# bootstrap wrote all three into this file in eae75ca. #27 removed them as dead
# entries — the same cleanup that cost us .gitattributes in PR #29 — and #30
# restored only .gitattributes. Bootstrap runs only when .fern/replay.lock is
# absent, so it will not re-add this one for us.
#
# .fern/replay.lock is deliberately NOT listed, measured rather than assumed:
# hedra-python does list it and Replay rewrote its lock anyway in the
# 2026-08-11 regeneration (current_generation dc24150), and hedra-cli does not
# list it and has never lost one — PR #29 deleted .gitattributes in the very
# same run that left .fern/replay.lock MODIFIED. The entry is inert in both
# directions. Losing the lock is also self-healing (Replay re-bootstraps the
# ledger from HEAD); losing replay.yml silently un-excludes the version stamps.
.fern/replay.yml

# Hand-written planning docs (ENG-10241). The generator emits nothing under
# docs/ — no such tree in fernapi/fern-cli-generator:0.30.2, and no regeneration
# commit has ever touched the directory — so this is load-bearing for the same
# reason tests/ above is. Until now the files' only protection was Replay
# re-adding them through patch-d871322c on every generation, which is also why
# that patch parks ~1.8k lines of markdown in replay.lock. This entry fixes both:
# Replay cleanses a patch whose files ALL match .fernignore, exactly as it did to
# patch-eeb99c81 (root .gitignore) once #60 added that entry.
docs/
